From eae57c77b7a271abcc371265be383efe66e6130a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew DeRosier <andy@skychase.zone>
Date: Wed, 26 Aug 2026 04:07:58 +0900
Subject: m68k: Add the near variable attribute

This patch adds `__attribute__ ((near))` which, in combination with
a specially configured linker script, emits working absolute word
addresses on M68000. This is common handwritten ASM trick used on
Mega Drive, which addresses RAM at the very top of address space
(0xFF0000 - 0xFFFFFF). Using a sign-extended word, the top 32KB are
addressable using (xxx).w instead of (xxx).l.

Near data goes in .sbss or .sdata. You must define these sections
in your linker script, ex:

    /* In MEMORY */
    RAM (rwx) : ORIGIN = 0xFFFF0000, LENGTH = 64K
    
    /* Globals */
    _stack_floor = 0xFFFFFC00;       /* 0x400 stack budget */
    _near_base = 0xFFFF8000;         /* lowest (sym).w reachable address */
    
    /* In SECTIONS */
    .sbss _near_base (NOLOAD) : {
        _near_start = .;
        *(.sbss .sbss.* .scommon .scommon.*)
        _near_end = .;
    } > RAM

    /* Trailing catch-all so the ASSERT below can see initialised near data */
    .sdata (NOLOAD) : { *(.sdata .sdata.*) } > RAM

    /* (A NEAR=0 build has an empty window and .bss is free to cross it.) */
    ASSERT(_near_end == _near_start || _bss_end <= _near_base, ".bss overlaps the near window")
    ASSERT(_near_end <= _stack_floor, "near data overlaps the stack budget")
    /* FIXME: Need to add a loop to the boot code to copy sdata to RAM */
    ASSERT(SIZEOF(.sdata) == 0, "near data with an initialiser has no ROM copy")

The ASSERTs are important; ld will not throw an error if near data
bleeds outside of the new sections, happily producing invalid code.

The near attribute must also be attached to externs of the variable,
or external modules will still use the long form.

Near data is not compatible with `-fpic`, the attribute will be ignred
when using position independent code.

This patch does not effect any existing code; it is inert without the
use of `__attribute__ ((near))`.

